<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oʻahu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oahu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oʻahu is the third-largest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a> and the most populous, home to the state capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikiki">Waikīkī</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>. Ruled by its own paramount chiefs, it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> in 1783 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> in 1795 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a>. Its sheltered harbours made it the commercial and political centre of the Kingdom of Hawaii in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oahu.jpg" alt="Oʻahu" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Sciences and Image Analysis, NASA-Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oahu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
